WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.060
Speaker 1: One of the really worrying trends
in the United States right now, really under

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:06.530
this new modern Republican Party.

00:00:06.530 --> 00:00:10.410
Call it whatever you want to jump in or whatever
the case may be.

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:14.500
The thing is that facts truly do not matter
anymore.

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:19.970
And rewriting history to suit whatever point
you're trying to make today, it has become

00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:21.960
a completely acceptable means to an end.

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:23.640
It doesn't matter how you rewrite history.

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:26.199
It doesn't matter what lie you tell about
the past.

00:00:26.199 --> 00:00:31.020
As long as it supports whatever point you're
trying to make today, it's OK for this modern

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:32.619
Republican Party.

00:00:32.619 --> 00:00:33.760
Here is one new example.

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:38.530
From the 4th of July weekend, Donald Trump's
former press secretary, Kayleigh McEnany,

00:00:38.530 --> 00:00:44.240
appeared on Fox News and the context was making
the point that conservatives are victims and

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:46.880
that Democrats are unpatriotic.

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:52.329
And Kaili wildly claimed that the founding
fathers, the main founding fathers, were all

00:00:52.329 --> 00:00:54.270
against slavery.

00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:55.270
This is a lie.

00:00:55.270 --> 00:00:56.710
It's just a big fat lie.

00:00:56.710 --> 00:00:57.890
And we're going to look at what she said.

00:00:57.890 --> 00:01:03.190
And the context was that Democratic Congresswoman
Corey Bush said that July 4th is sort of a

00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:04.759
celebration for white people.

00:01:04.759 --> 00:01:09.639
Corey Bush said when they say that the Fourth
of July is about American freedom, remember,

00:01:09.639 --> 00:01:13.789
the freedom they're referring to is for white
people in the sense that we are celebrating

00:01:13.789 --> 00:01:19.709
the anniversary of a country which at year
one, the date that is being celebrated was

00:01:19.709 --> 00:01:24.359
one in which black people were enslaved in
the United States.

00:01:24.359 --> 00:01:25.579
That's the context.

00:01:25.579 --> 00:01:29.350
OK, let's look at what Kayleigh McEnany had
to say about it,

00:01:29.350 --> 00:01:30.840
Speaker 5: starting with you, Bill Bennett.

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:33.599
The haters never take a day off from hating.

00:01:33.599 --> 00:01:34.599
That is clear.

00:01:34.599 --> 00:01:37.419
And they never take a day off of getting the
facts wrong.

00:01:37.419 --> 00:01:42.499
We know most of our forefathers, all of our
main founding fathers were against slavery.

00:01:42.499 --> 00:01:43.499
Recognize the evils of it.

00:01:43.499 --> 00:01:47.909
There's a great piece to that end on Heritage
called 1776, not sixteen 19.

00:01:47.909 --> 00:01:49.469
But I'll leave it to you.

00:01:49.469 --> 00:01:51.840
You know, they're so wrong and so much where
to begin.

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:53.889
Speaker 1: So that is completely untrue.

00:01:53.889 --> 00:01:57.700
OK, that's really, really, really not true.

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:01.909
Let's actually go through this because there's
there's an opportunity to actually learn a

00:02:01.909 --> 00:02:03.219
little something here.

00:02:03.219 --> 00:02:06.649
The majority of the founding fathers of the
United States owned slaves.

00:02:06.649 --> 00:02:09.939
Now, you could say, well, they owned them,
but they were against it.

00:02:09.939 --> 00:02:11.540
We're going to come back to that.

00:02:11.540 --> 00:02:16.799
OK, but but they were not against it enough
not to have their own slaves.

00:02:16.799 --> 00:02:22.850
Now, I'm talking about Benjamin Franklin,
John Hancock, John John Jay, Thomas Jefferson,

00:02:22.850 --> 00:02:25.659
James Madison, George Washington and many
others.

00:02:25.659 --> 00:02:26.980
They they owned slaves.

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:29.750
Some of the names you might not recognize
as readily.

00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:34.469
There were a few founding fathers that did
not own slaves, John Adams and Samuel Adams

00:02:34.469 --> 00:02:39.840
in Massachusetts, for example, Thomas Paine,
I don't believe is known to have owned slaves.

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:45.760
OK, so when Kayleigh McEnany says most of
the founding fathers were against slavery,

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:47.500
mostly they owned slaves.

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:54.159
Now, what is true is that over time, many
founding fathers, including some that owned

00:02:54.159 --> 00:02:59.370
slaves, started to recognize some problems
with slavery.

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:05.030
Some realized, rightly, that slavery was antithetical
to the concept of freedom and liberty on which

00:03:05.030 --> 00:03:08.060
the US was supposedly based.

00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:11.349
But some of them seemed more to be thinking
about it.

00:03:11.349 --> 00:03:12.650
Almost like economically.

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:18.980
Thomas Jefferson condemned the injustice of
the slave trade in the initial draft of the

00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:20.980
Declaration of Independence.

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:25.769
But there are counter points about whether
did that mean he was against slavery or did

00:03:25.769 --> 00:03:30.349
he just not like the slave trade as it was
being carried out internationally?

00:03:30.349 --> 00:03:34.770
So even that I mean, I'm sure people will
write in and say, no, David, that's super

00:03:34.770 --> 00:03:35.770
clear.

00:03:35.770 --> 00:03:37.030
It was either this or it was that.

00:03:37.030 --> 00:03:39.670
There's there's some ambiguity to it.

00:03:39.670 --> 00:03:40.920
But he owns slaves.

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:45.680
And then you had some who really wanted to
change nothing about whether slavery was federally

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:47.969
legal and just say it's a state issue.

00:03:47.969 --> 00:03:54.959
Now, if, for example, slave owner Benjamin
Franklin, slave owner owner Benjamin Franklin

00:03:54.959 --> 00:04:02.190
in Pennsylvania, supported anti slavery causes,
but not federally, you know, if if South Carolina

00:04:02.190 --> 00:04:04.780
wants to keep having slaves, will, that's
their thing.

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:07.829
But in Pennsylvania, we shouldn't have slaves
necessarily.

00:04:07.829 --> 00:04:13.140
So to call that being antislavery again, that
this is pretty nebulous in many ways.

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:19.449
So the point here is, was the opinion on slavery
of the founding fathers maybe more complex

00:04:19.449 --> 00:04:22.140
than they were either for it or against it?

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:23.160
Absolutely.

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:27.680
Did some founding fathers have seemingly inconsistent
positions on slavery?

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:28.680
Absolutely.

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:31.169
Did some of them change their views on slavery
over time?

00:04:31.169 --> 00:04:33.210
Yes, no doubt about it.

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:36.699
But as Kayleigh McEnany right when she says
most of the founding fathers were against

00:04:36.699 --> 00:04:40.550
slavery, no, that's a complete and total lie.

00:04:40.550 --> 00:04:47.090
And the point here of what Kayleigh McEnany
did was simply to try to attack Quarrie Bush

00:04:47.090 --> 00:04:52.910
by saying when Corie Bush says July 4th really
celebrates freedom for white people because

00:04:52.910 --> 00:04:58.670
at the time black people were enslaved, Kayleigh
wants to say, well, no, we're the founding

00:04:58.670 --> 00:04:59.670
fathers.

00:04:59.670 --> 00:05:05.539
We're all against slavery, and that is simply
inaccurate, and so her entire defense falls

00:05:05.539 --> 00:05:06.539
apart.

00:05:06.539 --> 00:05:14.229
Now, whether you agree that July 4th is a
pro slavery holiday or a celebration of an

00:05:14.229 --> 00:05:17.600
era when there was slavery, you know that
that's a debate.

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:22.169
That's a debate you can have and you can say,
well, OK, yes, we're celebrating the anniversary

00:05:22.169 --> 00:05:25.240
of a date at which there was slavery.

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:26.870
We no longer have that.

00:05:26.870 --> 00:05:31.110
We can still recognize that this is a milestone
in the existence of the country without being

00:05:31.110 --> 00:05:33.800
in agreement with all the things that the
country did at the time.

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:34.800
Right.

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:36.540
Then you're in the weeds, you're in the details.

00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:38.740
But Kayleigh McEnany is just wrong.

00:05:38.740 --> 00:05:43.419
And this is the exact type of little pithy
talking point that the average Fox News viewer

00:05:43.419 --> 00:05:47.169
will never investigate and will simply start
to repeat and repeat and repeat.

00:05:47.169 --> 00:07:09.590
It's sad more than anything else.

